<Base lang="en" title="100 Small Things About Toronto"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>100 Small Things About Toronto.</h1><p>A published essay collection: one hundred small entries plus a set of Montreal notes, written with no outline — the recurring threads only became visible in hindsight. A few of the lighter selections are below.</p><div class="ep-layout toronto-ep-layout"><div class="ep-tabs"><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="1">Ep.34 The Sandwich Longer Than My Arm</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="2">Ep.47 Turns Out There Is Such a Thing as a Free Lunch</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="3">Ep.20 My Taste Buds Are Saved!</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="4">Ep.2 The Strange Taxi</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="5">Ep.9 Ice Latte and Double Double</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="6">Ep.16 The Yellow Centipede and the Red Tin Box</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="8">Ep.18 The Good Eats of Kensington Market</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="9">Ep.30 The Vaughan Mills Heist</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="11">Ep.51 Center Island and Those Contemplations</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="13">Ep.60 A Racetrack Called the 401</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="14">Ep.55 Thirty Bucks for This?!</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="15">Ep.56 Wait? Our School Has That?</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="16">Ep.19 Why always me?!</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="17">Ep.7 So, What's Syna's Type?</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="18">Ep.66 That Day's Double Rainbow</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="20">Read the Full Book</button></div><div class="ep-panels"><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="1"><p>After the immense shock of the medium-sized Coke at Wendy's, the next thing to inspire in me a profound reverence for the concept of "North American Sizing" was the legendary "footlong" sub from Subway.</p><p>That afternoon, Ivy and I had just been liberated from a sleep-inducing accounting class and were discussing what to have for dinner.</p><p>"Syna, I'll treat you to dinner today," Ivy said, suddenly pulling a stack of papers from her backpack and waving them in front of me like a nouveau riche tycoon who had just struck it rich.</p><p>I leaned in to look. It was a stack of beautifully printed coupons, colorfully adorned with the logos and discount information of various fast-food chains.</p><p>"Where did you get these?" I asked curiously. In my world, coupons were something you usually had to actively seek out or snag on an app.</p><p>"Oh, they just came in the mail," she replied nonchalantly, as if it were as common as receiving a utility bill.</p><p>But why had I, living in the dorm, never received these in my mailbox? I felt like I had missed out on a billion dollars.</p><p>"Look at this!" Ivy said, pulling one out like she was presenting a treasure. "Subway, buy one footlong, get one free!"</p><p>I knew Subway; we have it back home, and its main selling point is being healthy. It sounded good, and a buy-one-get-one-free deal was a godsend for a frugal international student.</p><p>"Let's go!" I was immediately swept away by the word "free" and happily agreed.</p><p>At the Subway near the university, a peculiar smell of baked bread and various vegetables hit me. Ivy and I stood in line. While I was still studying the dense options on the menu, Ivy had already skillfully begun her order.</p><p>"A footlong, on Italian herbs and cheese bread. Teriyaki chicken, toasted. Provolone cheese," she rattled off, and then the staff member's gaze turned to me.</p><p>My turn. I instantly fell into the same panic as when I first ordered coffee. Which bread? Which meat? Which cheese? And that long row of colorful vegetables and sauces behind them! This wasn't ordering food; it was clearly a pop quiz!</p><p>"The... the same as hers, please." Faced with immense pressure of choice, I shamefully gave up.</p><p>Then, I watched as the employee pulled out something that looked like a baguette from the bread cabinet and sliced it open down the middle with a long knife.</p><p>My heart skipped a beat.</p><p>A footlong. That's 30.48 centimeters! It was almost longer than my forearm! I stared blankly as the employee stuffed that giant loaf with chicken and vegetables as if building a wall, finally squeezing on several lines of sauce. By the time it was wrapped in paper, it had become a heavy, thick, veritable "submarine."</p><p>When Ivy and I each sat down with a "submarine," I fully understood the sense of absurdity I had felt at Wendy's at the beginning of the semester.</p><p>I needed both hands just to hold the sandwich, feeling like a hamster clutching a giant ear of corn. I opened my mouth as wide as I could but could only manage to bite off a small corner of its cross-section. Meanwhile, Ivy next to me had already effortlessly demolished a quarter of hers, eating with great satisfaction.</p><p>In that moment, I finally had an epiphany. In North America, the concept of a "single serving" was probably not what I understood it to be. The "serving for one" here was designed for a 200-pound man.</p><p>That meal, I tried my best and still only managed to eat half of my "arm-length sandwich." I took the other half back to the dorm, heated it in the microwave the next day, and had it for lunch.</p><p>From then on, whenever I saw a coupon that said "Free," I would shudder. Because you never know what a heavy, sweet burden is hidden behind that "free" offer.</p><p>(Excuse me ????)</p><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep34-arm-sandwich.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep34-arm-sandwich.jpg" alt="The giant sandwich next to an arm" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>The sandwich, held up next to an arm for scale.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="2"><p>Besides being the dorm's gossip center, Karen was actually a hidden "cooking fanatic" just like me. But since her family is Hindu, her culinary habits were rich with South Asian flair.</p><p>At first, due to certain stereotypes, I kept my distance from all Indian food. I always thought of it as a mysterious dark cuisine made up of countless spices I couldn't name and mushy textures.</p><p>Until one time, Karen showed off her skills in the dorm kitchen. She made a curry with chickpeas, potatoes, and various spices, served with fragrant rice. I took a bite with the attitude of "I'll risk my life to accompany a friend," and was instantly conquered by the rich, layered flavors of the spices. It turned out that real Indian food was completely different from what I had imagined! From then on, I became a loyal fan of Karen's cooking.</p><p>"What's this?" One day, I received a screenshot of a poster from Karen on Instagram. The design was simple, with a few cartoon vegetables and the words "Community Kitchen: Learn to Cook Healthy Meals!"</p><p>"It's a free cooking class at school," Karen sent in a voice message, her tone filled with the excitement of discovering a treasure. "I asked around and heard that you can take the food you cook for free right on the spot!"</p><p>The word "free," like a bolt of lightning, struck me right in the heart. As a poor and greedy international student, what could be more attractive than "free food"?</p><p>So, on a sunny Wednesday afternoon, Karen and I, two "foodie partners in crime," cheerfully headed to the "free lunch" site in the basement cafeteria.</p><p>It was a large area cordoned off by square tables, already filled with students. There were blond, blue-eyed local students, as well as international students like us from all over the world. A young white guy in a tall chef's hat was smilingly handing out aprons and recipes to everyone.</p><p>Today's theme was—Vietnamese Spring Rolls.</p><p>Karen and I received our "battle supplies": translucent rice paper, fresh shrimp, thin rice noodles, and a large pile of refreshing-looking vegetables like lettuce, mint leaves, and shredded carrots.</p><p>The process was much more complicated than I had imagined. It wasn't just about chopping and cooking; it was more like a delicate craft class. The hardest part was handling the dry, brittle rice paper. The chef told us to soak it in warm water for a few seconds until it became soft and transparent, but not too soft, or it would stick together into an unsalvageable tragedy.</p><p>I carefully placed the first sheet of rice paper into the water, silently counting "one, two, three," then clumsily pulled it out. The result was either still hard on one side or already a mushy mess on the other. Karen, on the other hand, once again showed her "cooking fanatic" talent, handling every sheet of rice paper perfectly.</p><p>The kitchen was filled with exclamations and laughter in various languages. Someone would wail because their rice paper tore, while another would cheer for successfully rolling their first spring roll. It felt less like a class and more like a lively and challenging craft party.</p><p>When we carefully wrapped the cooked shrimp, rice noodles, and various shredded vegetables in the soft rice paper, a translucent spring roll, faintly revealing the colorful fillings inside, was born in our hands.</p><p>"Okay, everyone! Time to enjoy the meal you made!" the chef announced loudly.</p><p>Karen and I took our plate of incredibly professional-looking Vietnamese spring rolls, paired with a special peanut sauce, and found a seat by the window. I picked one up, dipped it in the sauce, and took the first bite. The rice paper was soft and chewy, the shrimp sweet, the vegetables refreshing, and the aroma of mint exploded in my mouth, all complemented by the rich peanut sauce…</p><p>It was so delicious!</p><p>I looked at the students coming and going outside the window, then at the substantial, delicious, and most importantly—completely free—lunch in my hands, my heart filled with a huge sense of satisfaction and happiness.</p><p>It was then that I understood that the saying my parents taught me since I was a child, "There's no such thing as a free lunch," didn't hold true at this magical Canadian university.</p><p>Here, there was not only a free lunch, but also free cooking lessons, and the priceless joy of sharing food with friends.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="3"><p>If universities back home are backed byvibrant snack streets, then North American universities can only be described as "barren."</p><p>When I was still back home, the back alleys and snack streets of my university were my source of joy. There was steaming hot Malatang, sizzling Teppanyaki, fragrant grilled cold noodles, and the ever-popular Takoyaki with its perpetual queue. Every small stall was a portal to a comforting world of deliciousness.</p><p>But after arriving in Toronto, I discovered that the "cuisine" around the university was basically monopolized by a few fast-food giants. Tim Hortons, Subway, Pizza Pizza... they were like tireless copy-paste templates, occupying every prime location.</p><p>Eating them once or twice felt new, but after a solid week, I felt like my taste buds were being completely formatted.</p><p>Just when I thought my university life was destined to be a cycle of burgers, sandwiches, and donuts, I discovered the inconspicuous little street behind the university.</p><p>There were no bright signs of chain stores, only a few small, slightly worn-down storefronts. And the ones that left the deepest impression on me were the tiny sushi shop and the Kabab place next to it.</p><p>The sushi shop was so small that it only had one counter and two or three tables. The owners were a quiet Chinese couple. They didn't talk much and always wore gentle smiles. The husband was in charge of making sushi behind the counter, while the wife took orders and packed them.</p><p>Their movements weren't fast, but every step was filled with care. The first time I went, I ordered a simple salmon sushi. The owner took out a whole piece of fish from the cooler and, with a long knife, deftly sliced off a few pieces. The texture of the fish glistened freshly under the light.</p><p>He placed the fish slice on the warm rice and, with a gentle squeeze, a perfect piece of sushi was born. I put it in my mouth, and the richness of the fish and the slight tartness of the rice melted together. In that moment, I felt healed.</p><p>Later, that little sushi shop became my secret spot for treating myself. Oh, and besides the regular sushi, their poke bowls were also exceptional. Even after I graduated and moved to an off-campus apartment, I would still often order takeout from them on Uber Eats. It was a familiar comfort etched into my taste buds.</p><p>The Kabab shop next door, however, had a completely different vibe. The owner was an enthusiastic Middle Eastern man, always wearing an apron stained with sauce, who would greet every student loudly in his heavily accented English.</p><p>His shop was always filled with a powerful, rich aroma of grilled meat and various spices. Giant skewers of meat sizzled on the grill, the fat dripping onto the charcoal and sending up puffs of white smoke.</p><p>His shop was a blessing for all hungry students because it had one defining characteristic: huge portions, and cheap.</p><p>My usual order was the Chicken with Rice. For under twenty dollars (the price has probably gone up by now), the owner would give you a giant container piled high with golden spiced rice, topped with a mountain of grilled chicken, and served with a large portion of salad and golden-brown naan bread.</p><p>The portion was so substantial it could satisfy any big guy. Usually, I could split one dinner into two meals; reheating it in the microwave the next day made for another hearty lunch.</p><p>And so, one quiet, one lively; one delicate, one hearty. These two small shops, like two silent guardians, held up a space for us students, surrounded by bland campus food and fast food, to breathe—a world filled with human warmth and authentic flavors.</p><p>They were the most delicious and the brightest stars on my barren campus food map.</p><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep20-kabab.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep20-kabab.jpg" alt="A meal from the Kabab shop behind school" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>A meal from the Kabab shop behind school.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="4"><p>One thing about Canada is that they don't skimp on the air conditioning, not on the planes and certainly not in the airports.</p><p>Having just arrived from a connecting flight in Vancouver, the moment I stepped through the terminal's automatic doors, I was hit by a wave of early September heat that made my whole body jolt. The flush of embarrassment from the stuck wheel incident hadn't even faded from my cheeks. Now, as the hot wind hit my face, it created a wretched sensation, like being caught between fire and ice.</p><p>I stared silently at the 'No Service' indicator on my phone. It took a few restarts, but finally, the 4G signal flickered back to life. Breathing a sigh of relief, I followed the "Taxi" signs and, after waiting for about ten minutes in a somewhat chaotic curbside line, it was finally my turn.</p><p>The driver was a South Asian man with dark skin and a scruffy beard. He wordlessly helped me stuff my two giant suitcases into the trunk, his movements efficient, though he never once looked at me.</p><p>The moment I pulled the car door open, I froze.</p><p>Perhaps because the pandemic wasn't truly over, a thick plexiglass divider, stretching nearly to the roof, separated the back seats from the front.</p><p>In the middle of the divider was a small, semi-circular cutout, just large enough for a credit card or cash to pass through. The entire surface was covered in fine scratches, which, in the dim light of the car's interior, looked like a frozen, shattered spiderweb.</p><p>As I got in and shut the door, I felt less like I was in a taxi and more like I was being locked in a moving, airtight, transparent cage.</p><p>Since the school term hadn't started yet, I couldn't get into my dorm despite our email exchanges with the school. Down on my luck, I had no choice but to book a hotel as a temporary residence hastily.</p><p>I gave the driver the hotel address. He just glanced at me in the rearview mirror, grunted an indistinct 'uh-huh,' and started the car.</p><p>The radio played music in a language I couldn't understand, its fast-paced melody and chaotic drum beats sounding like a string of mumbled incantations, making the small backseat space feel even more confined. The car quickly entered the highway. I had expected a swift journey, but after only a few minutes, our speed dropped, and eventually, we came to a complete stop in the endless stream of traffic.</p><p>I peered out the window to see a frozen river formed by countless red taillights.</p><p>Cars were packed bumper-to-bumper, blanketing my entire field of vision with no end in sight.</p><p>In the distance, the city skyline was slowly sharpening in the morning light. I could even see the iconic spire of the CN Tower, but it was like a mirage—distant, unreal, its poetry dissolved by the frustration of the traffic jam and the silence inside the car.</p><p>My first real look at Toronto wasn't a postcard view. It was a distant, blurry silhouette seen through a dirty window while stuck in traffic on Highway 407.</p><p>"Is the traffic in Toronto always this bad?" I finally asked my first question since landing.</p><p>But no one answered.</p><p>The driver seemed very patient, or perhaps just used to it. One of his hands rested on the steering wheel, while the other tapped lightly on his leg to the rhythm of the radio.</p><p>He just sat there quietly on the other side of the divider, more like a programmed automaton than a person one could talk to.</p><p>A few times, I wanted to ask how much longer we'd be stuck, but the words died on my lips as I looked at that cold, impassive barrier.</p><p>That sheet of plexiglass was more concrete, more direct than the glass wall at the airport. It didn't just separate the space; it seemed to seal off language, warmth, and any possibility of connection.</p><p>My second encounter with this city was this experience of being enclosed, isolated, and immobilized.</p><p>I don't know how much time passed before the traffic finally began to crawl forward. When the car at last exited the highway and turned onto the city streets, I felt as if I were waking from a long dream. Outside the window, rows of unfamiliar shop signs and red-brick buildings flashed by.</p><p>And I was just a silent passenger in a transparent cage, about to be delivered to some random corner of this vast city.</p><p>"Here we are."</p><p>Dazed, it took me a moment to realize we had arrived. But as I stared at the dilapidated bungalow before me, I couldn't for the life of me believe this was the supposed four-star hotel I'd seen online.</p><p>I passed a hundred-dollar bill through the small cutout to the driver.</p><p>The driver gave me a suspicious look. He then started fumbling for something, muttering to himself.</p><p>I watched as he pulled his wallet from the armrest, digging out change while murmuring:</p><p>"This should be right."</p><p>The driver handed me the change. After confirming the amount was correct, I nodded at him.</p><p>The driver expertly killed the engine, parked, opened the trunk, and took out my suitcases.</p><p>"Alright, have a great day."</p><p>The black sedan turned and vanished around the corner, leaving me alone to wrestle with my two large suitcases in the wind.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="5"><p>Of course, any international student living in Canada knows that if there's a national beverage chain, the answer is, and can only be, Tim Hortons.</p><p>In Toronto, the existence of Tim Hortons is as natural as air and water. It's ubiquitous. On every street corner, at every gas station, in the lobby of every university building, you can see that familiar red sign.</p><p>It's not the kind of trendy coffee shop you make a special trip to for a photo op; it's more like an old friend, always there, offering you an affordable cup of coffee and a cloyingly sweet donut.</p><p>The first time I walked into a Tim's, I was practically dragged in by Leia and Karen on either side. It was an early morning, and we were rushing to an 8:30 AM class.</p><p>"Come on, Syna, let's go get some coffee to keep us alive," Karen said.</p><p>While waiting in line, I was still debating whether to get a latte or an Americano. However, I soon discovered that the ordering process here seemed to follow a secret code I wasn't privy to.</p><p>A construction worker in front of us said crisply to the cashier, "A large Double-Double, please."</p><p>Without looking up, the cashier operated the coffee machine, the movements seamless.</p><p>The guy in a baseball jersey before him used the same lingo: "A medium Double-Double."</p><p>Double-Double? What was that? A special kind of coffee bean? A seasonal secret menu item? I felt like a civilian who had stumbled into a spy rendezvous, surrounded by people communicating in a code I couldn't understand.</p><p>It was my turn. Faced with this huge gap in my knowledge, I chickened out and ordered the only safe option I recognized:</p><p>"An Iced Latte, please."</p><p>The cashier glanced at me, and in that look, there seemed to be a subtle, almost imperceptible judgment reserved for an amateur.</p><p>I took my iced latte and found a table with Leia and Karen. Both of their cups had a small "DD" printed on them.</p><p>Leia was about to take a sip of her coffee. Hearing my question, she paused for a second, then laughed. "Oh, that's just double cream, double sugar."</p><p>"That's it?" I was stunned.</p><p>"Yeah," Karen added from the side. "If you want one cream and one sugar, you just say 'Regular.' If you don't want anything, you say 'Black.' It's super convenient."</p><p>I looked at the iced latte in my hand, then at the "double cream, double sugar" in their cups, and felt like a complete idiot. I had spent twice as much money on a perfectly ordinary iced latte, while they, with a simple piece of slang, had gotten the most authentic Canadian national coffee.</p><p>It was then that I understood. The essence of Tim Hortons wasn't about how good the coffee was, but about its "language system"—a system built for efficiency that was deeply integrated into the daily lives of Canadians.</p><p>It's not fancy, it's not sophisticated, but it's fast enough, simple enough, to give every Canadian rushing to work or school a direct dose of calories and comfort in the shortest possible time.</p><p>Later, I tried ordering a "Double-Double" myself. The taste, how to put it... was just straightforwardly sweet, with the aroma of creamer, having little to do with the actual bitterness of the coffee.</p><p>But every time I see that familiar red sign on a cold winter day or a drowsy morning, I remember that morning, and the mysterious "secret code" that gave me my first real taste of cultural difference—</p><p>"Double-Double."</p><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep9-tim-hortons.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep9-tim-hortons.jpg" alt="An iced coffee from Tim Hortons" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>An iced coffee from Tim Hortons, held up at the intersection.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="6"><p>Besides the red city buses and the regional buses (like YRT in the York Region) that run all over the streets, another crucial part of Toronto's public transit system is the shabby, vintage-looking subway and streetcars.</p><p>The first time I took the subway, I was amused by the giant route map in the station.</p><p>The yellow Line 1, unlike the clear and straightforward subway lines back home, was a giant U-shape, like a winding yellow centipede crawling to some unknown destination.</p><p>The green Line 2 was relatively normal, just a standard straight line running east to west.</p><p>But the most comical of all was the purple Line 4—the entire line consisted of just four short stops. Every time I saw it, I felt it wasn't so much a subway line as an unfinished draft that had been abandoned halfway through planning.</p><p>But the bizarre map was just the beginning. As I sat on the rickety train, about to message Ivy who was waiting for me at the final station, I quickly discovered another anti-human design—there was no signal on the subway!</p><p>At the top of my phone screen, the signal icon went from five full bars to "No Service" in an instant.</p><p>I stared helplessly at the glaring red exclamation mark next to the unsent message in my chat window, wildly imagining Ivy stomping her feet impatiently on the platform, all while pondering why on earth humanity would invent something like a subway with no signal.</p><p>Not only that, but while the Toronto subway was far less crowded than during rush hour back home, the cars always had a unique "atmosphere." You could always count on encountering an "artist" blasting music without a care in the world, or a homeless person quietly emitting a mysterious odor in a corner.</p><p>It was a complex, one-of-a-kind "TTC ambiance," co-created by the aging facilities and the diverse array of passengers.</p><p>Of course, in places like downtown, Toronto still preserves an even older "intangible cultural heritage"—the streetcar.</p><p>The red, tin-box-like cars trundled slowly along tracks in the middle of the road, with two long "pigtails" trailing from the roof, connected to overhead electrical wires.</p><p>The first time I rode one and pressed the red button to request a stop, I felt like I had time-traveled back to a world at least twenty years behind my home country.</p><p>It waited at red lights with all the other cars and would get stuck in traffic at intersections, unable to move. Its only advantage, perhaps, was its retro, clanging stop announcement bell, which added a slow-paced, frustratingly nostalgic feel to the bustling city.</p><div class="ep-photos"><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-subway-crowd.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-subway-crowd.jpg" alt="The escalator crowd at York University station" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>The escalator crowd at York University subway station.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-shuttle-notice.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-shuttle-notice.jpg" alt="TTC weekend shuttle-bus closure notice" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>A notice for a weekend subway closure, replaced by shuttle buses.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-streetcar.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep16-streetcar.jpg" alt="A red streetcar at the Spadina intersection" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>A red streetcar at the Spadina intersection.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="8"><p>I think I'm the embodiment of a foodie. As the old saying goes, food is heaven, and I'd heard that Toronto has some of the best Chinese and various Asian cuisines in all of North America. As a gourmand, how could I possibly miss out?</p><p>After growing tired of the barren food scene around the university, I set my next exploration target on the legendary "treasure trove"—Kensington Market.</p><p>Kensington Market is unlike any other place in Toronto. There are no skyscrapers, no chain stores, only rows of colorful Victorian houses covered in bold graffiti.</p><p>The air is always filled with a complex, peculiar aroma—a mixture of coffee, spices, old books, and various street foods.</p><p>I was completely captivated on my first visit. This place is a paradise for an Asian stomach.</p><p>At the entrance of an inconspicuous little shop, I saw a familiar Cantonese sign. I peered closer and, my goodness, it was rice noodle rolls (Cheung Fun)!</p><p>I immediately ordered a shrimp rice noodle roll. When the server brought it over, cut into small pieces and drizzled with a special soy sauce, I couldn't wait to spear a piece with my fork. The smooth, tender texture, full of the aroma of rice, paired with the savory soy sauce and the springy shrimp, instantly reminded me of the streetside breakfast stalls back home.</p><p>After finishing the rice noodle rolls, I turned into another alley, drawn by the rich aroma of beef bone broth. It was a small Pho restaurant, always packed with people.</p><p>I ordered a classic Pho Dac Biet (a special combination). When the steaming bowl was served, I took a deep breath. The complex fragrance, simmered from star anise, cinnamon, and beef bones, instantly dispelled all my fatigue.</p><p>I pushed the bean sprouts and basil into the hot broth, squeezed in some lime juice, and added a spoonful of Sriracha. A sip of broth, a bite of noodles, a piece of meat—that warm and rich flavor soothed me all the way from my tongue to my stomach.</p><p>Of course, next door to the Pho place was what could only be described as the "savior of international students"—the Banh Mi shop. For just four or five dollars (the price has probably gone up by now), you could get a huge sandwich on a crispy baguette, stuffed with grilled pork, pâté, pickled daikon, cilantro, and chili peppers.</p><p>The taste—sour, sweet, spicy, salty, and fragrant—was a multi-layered explosion in my mouth. The value for money was criminally good.</p><p>After such a feast, how could I skip dessert? Tucked away at the other end of the market was a low-key Thai dessert stall, and its star was the unforgettable mango sticky rice.</p><p>Warm scoops of sticky rice, fresh from the pot, were bathed in fragrant coconut milk, soft and glutinous with a gentle sweetness. Beside it were freshly cut ripe mangoes, golden and aromatic. A final drizzle of slightly salty coconut cream added a soft contrast to the whole experience.</p><p>Every bite was a fusion of creamy and sweet, as if happiness itself had been made edible.</p><p>Walking out of Kensington Market, I was carrying my leftover Banh Mi, the sweet taste of mango sticky rice still lingering in my mouth. It was then that I truly understood why so many people call Toronto a food capital.</p><p>Its charm isn't in the high-end Michelin restaurants, but precisely in corners like Kensington Market, filled with a lively, down-to-earth atmosphere that embraces flavors from all over the world. This place was the promised land for a foodie like me.</p><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep18-pho.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep18-pho.jpg" alt="A bowl of Vietnamese pho" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>A bowl of pho, eaten in Kensington Market.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="9"><p>Any international student living in North York (or near Vaughan) knows that if Toronto is the "newbie village" for students, then Vaughan Mills Outlet is our own personal "armory" and "supply depot."</p><p>In Toronto, prices are high and the tax rate is touching. Every time you spend money, it feels like you're cutting off a piece of your own flesh. But at Vaughan Mills, the scene changes completely. This isn't shopping; it's treasure hunting, it's a battle, an unspoken game between you and the major brands to see who can get the better deal.</p><p>The first time I was dragged to Vaughan Mills by my roommate Anne was on an unremarkable weekend. She said to me in a mysterious "I'll show you the world" tone, "I'm taking you to a good place."</p><p>As soon as I walked into the huge, circular mall, I was stunned by the bustling atmosphere. Everyone here was walking briskly, with sharp eyes, carrying bags of all sizes, their faces beaming with the joy of a fruitful hunt.</p><p>Our first target was the Nike factory store. Back home, a new pair of Nikes could easily cost over a thousand RMB. But here, I watched with my own eyes as a pair of classic Air Force 1s had a price tag that clearly read: $79.99. I rubbed my eyes and looked closer to make sure there wasn't a hidden decimal point after the "7." Eighty dollars, and Canadian dollars at that! I felt my sense of value being violently shaken. This wasn't buying shoes; this was practically a giveaway!</p><p>However, the truly "god-tier" moment happened when we were preparing for the coming winter. Toronto's winter is the kind that can make you freeze to the point of questioning your existence. Before I came, I had heard that a Canada Goose jacket was standard issue. But when I saw the price tag, often over a thousand dollars, my poor soul felt a deep sting.</p><p>Just when I thought I was destined to spend the winter wrapped in three layers of Uniqlo, Anne took me into an outdoor brand store. In the deepest part of the sales section hung a row of unremarkable-looking down jackets. I casually picked one up, looked at the tag, and it read: "Withstands extreme weather down to -40°C." Then I looked at the price tag: $199.99.</p><p>Two hundred dollars. To withstand minus forty degrees.</p><p>I don't know how many times cheaper it was than a Canada Goose, I only know that in that moment, it shone with a holy light in my eyes. This jacket didn't represent fashion or a brand; it represented survival, dignity, the hope of a poor international student to live on in a harsh foreign land!</p><p>Of course, the surprises at Vaughan Mills didn't end there. During a summer sale, I even managed to find a T-shirt for only $30 in the Lululemon discount section. Lululemon! The middle-class yoga dream that was usually far out of reach for mere mortals like me! Thirty dollars, the price of a bubble tea and a dessert, and I actually owned it!</p><p>It was late when we left Vaughan Mills. Anne and I were loaded down with our spoils. Though our wallets were empty, our hearts were filled with an unprecedented sense of abundance.</p><p>Those moments of "robbing money" weren't robbing my money, but the brands' "lives." Every successful deal felt like winning a small victory medal for myself in this survival game of student life abroad.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="11"><p>If I had to choose my favorite place from Toronto's collection of sometimes shabby, inefficient, and maddening spots, it would undoubtedly be Center Island.</p><p>It's like a gentle, hidden back door to this huge, bustling, and sometimes indifferent city—a place to catch your breath.</p><p>To get there, you have to take a slow ferry from the downtown terminal. I love that process. When the ferry sounds its horn and slowly pulls away from the harbor, you feel yourself physically and ceremoniously bidding farewell to the headaches of schoolwork, bills, and social pressures.</p><p>The city's clamor is left further and further behind, replaced by the wind off Lake Ontario and the cries of water birds skimming the surface.</p><p>During that ten-minute journey, Toronto's skyline becomes a constantly changing, magnificent backdrop. The skyscrapers that usually feel oppressive, piercing the clouds, now become silent, exquisite silhouettes.</p><p>Looking at them, you feel like a chess piece that has temporarily escaped the board, gaining a brief, god-like perspective.</p><p>The moment you step onto the island, it's as if the world's volume has been turned down. There are no car engines, no maddening TTC announcements, only the rustle of wind through the leaves and the distant laughter of children.</p><p>Vast, neatly trimmed lawns spread out like green velvet carpets, inviting every weary soul to lie down.</p><p>On summer evenings, this place becomes a "second cafeteria" for University of Toronto students. You'll see groups of young people sitting on picnic blankets, sharing pizza and beer, playing frisbee, and laughing loudly, the air thick with the hormones of youth.</p><p>Even though I was also a student in the prime of my life, I usually didn't join them. I would just watch from a distance, feeling like an audience member watching a coming-of-age film.</p><p>I always came alone.</p><p>I would find a spot under a large tree on an empty patch of grass and sit down, taking my iPad and Apple Pencil from my backpack. This was my secret studio, where I created a different, happy ending for the characters I wrote about, one that diverged from the official canon.</p><p>I'd open my notes app, and the character I was currently "saving" would appear on the screen. Perhaps in the original story, his fate was tragic; perhaps he sacrificed himself for some grand goal; or perhaps he was ultimately separated from his beloved. But with my pen, all of this could be rewritten.</p><p>I would immerse myself in that world, and time would seem to stand still. For the character covered in wounds, I'd use my pen to give him a warm new coat; for the lovers forced apart, I'd add a scene of them walking hand-in-hand in the sun; I'd modify their dialogue, adjust their expressions, and with every stroke, I'd smooth over the regrets left by the official story that I couldn't let go of.</p><p>It's a strange, almost god-like experience. In real life, I can't control my grades, predict my future, or even decide if it will blizzard tomorrow. But here, on this small, glowing screen, I am the only god. I can decide everyone's fate, and for every virtual soul I deeply love, I can create a gentle, hopeful ending.</p><p>This is perhaps the most luxurious comfort I can give them, and myself.</p><p>When I pull myself out of that virtual world, it is usually dusk. The sun begins to set, and the summer sunset, like an overturned paint palette, dyes the entire sky in a magnificent gradient from orange-red to pinkish-purple.</p><p>I'd pack up my iPad and walk to the island's shore, looking across the shimmering lake at Toronto on the other side.</p><p>The skyline, formed by the CN Tower and the countless financial office buildings on Bay Street, looked as beautiful as an unreal painting against the sunset. Every glass curtain wall reflected the afterglow, as if the entire city had been plated with a warm, golden edge. It looked so prosperous, so successful, so full of infinite possibilities.</p><p>Nearby, parents were playing by the water with their laughing children, their laughter as crisp as wind chimes. They were carefree, and their futures, like the glowing city before them, seemed full of hope.</p><p>And me?</p><p>I looked at that magnificent city silhouette and then thought about myself.</p><p>My grades were less than ideal; I always played the most inconspicuous role in group projects. My abilities were mediocre; I had neither Ivy's innate artistic talent nor Leia and Karen's fearless social skills. Even in the area I was best at, I was just a reclusive otaku immersed in my own world, making small edits for virtual characters.</p><p>To come to this city, I had spent all my parents' savings and carried their expectations. I studied frantically here, experienced all kinds of culture shock here, and clumsily learned how to be an independent adult here.</p><p>But then what?</p><p>Where was my future, really?</p><p>Could I graduate successfully? Could I find a decent job? Could I find a small place that was truly mine in this seemingly prosperous city? Or would I, in the end, just be a passing guest, returning to where I came from a few years later with a diploma and a pile of complicated memories, to start all over again?</p><p>These questions, like the mist rising from the lake at dusk, silently yet pervasively enveloped me. I felt that between me and the glittering city on the other shore lay an insurmountable, cold expanse of water.</p><p>It was so close I could see the outline of every building.</p><p>And yet it was so far, so far that I didn't know how to reach it.</p><p>I stood on the shore for a long time, until the last glimmer of the sunset was swallowed by the night, and the city lights came on one by one, like a cold, starry sky reflected on the ground.</p><p>I turned and walked towards the ferry terminal for the return trip.</p><p>Center Island didn't give me any answers. It just gave me a safe, undisturbed space to face these questions. It was like a gentle, silent friend, offering me a quiet sanctuary to escape to when I was suffocating under the weight of reality.</p><p>The ferry sounded its horn again, slowly sailing towards that vast city of countless lights, a city full of the unknown and challenges.</p><p>I knew that tomorrow, I would be back to dealing with those headaches of schoolwork and bills.</p><p>But at least for tonight, I had had the tranquility of an entire island, and a period of time that was all my own, where I could freely create happy endings.</p><p>And that was enough.</p><div class="ep-photos"><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-centre-island.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-centre-island.jpg" alt="Looking back at the Toronto skyline from the island ferry" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>Looking back at the Toronto skyline from the island ferry.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-ferry-skyline.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-ferry-skyline.jpg" alt="The skyline from another island ferry ride" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>The skyline from another island ferry ride.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-toronto-sign.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep51-toronto-sign.jpg" alt="The Toronto sign at Nathan Phillips Square" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>The Toronto sign at Nathan Phillips Square.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="13"><p>Back home, I always thought highways were synonymous with order and rules. Clear speed limit signs, strict cameras, everyone driving properly in their own lane—overtaking was a major decision that required careful consideration.</p><p>Until I came to Toronto and got on Highway 401.</p><p>Only then did I understand that here, the speed limit signs on the highway are perhaps just a polite suggestion, or maybe, a decoration for reference only.</p><p>And the guides who introduced me to this new world of "fast and furious" were my roommate Karen and my friend Ivy. They both came from the famous "Racing Capital"—Brampton. It’s said that people from Brampton don’t have blood in their veins; they have gasoline.</p><p>The first time I was in Karen’s car on the 401, my entire understanding of "driving" was overturned. It was a weekend, and we were heading to a Costco a bit further away. The moment we entered the on-ramp and merged into the steel river of countless vehicles, Karen’s whole aura changed.</p><p>Her usual cheerful, extraverted persona vanished, replaced by a "God of Racing" with sharp eyes and one hand on the steering wheel.</p><p>The sign clearly said the speed limit was 120 km/h, but she hit the gas and easily pushed it to 140. And in the far-left express lane, that was just the "entry-level" speed. Cars zipped past us like bullets, so close I could even see the stubble on the other drivers’ faces.</p><p>"Karen, don’t you think that is too fast?" I asked, clutching the seatbelt so tightly my knuckles turned white.</p><p>"Fast? This is the normal speed," she replied nonchalantly, while seamlessly signaling, changing lanes, and overtaking in one fluid motion. "If you drive 120 on the 401, you’ll be honked to death."</p><p>Later, my parents came to visit me in Toronto. My dad, an "old pro" with decades of driving experience back home known for his steady driving, rented a car and ambitiously prepared to challenge the 401.</p><p>The result: his first time on it, he nearly had an existential crisis.</p><p>We were in the middle lane, and he was strictly adhering to the 120 km/h speed limit, but we became the slowest "obstacle" in the entire flow of traffic. Cars on both sides overtook us like a tidal wave, and those giant, Optimus Prime-like transport trucks tailgated us menacingly.</p><p>"Aren’t they afraid of the police?" my dad asked, his palms sweating as he gripped the steering wheel, his eyes darting between the three mirrors.</p><p>"Dad, I think... it’s a bit different here," I said from the passenger seat, my own heart pounding.</p><p>That time, a short half-hour drive felt like a fierce battle for both of us. When we got off the highway, my dad let out a long sigh of relief and said, "This isn’t a highway, it’s a racetrack."</p><p>From then on, I understood that Highway 401 has its own set of survival rules. Here, the rules aren’t written on the signs; they’re determined by the speed of the traffic. You can’t be slow, because being slow means being in danger.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="14"><p>As a student without a car, relying solely on my own two feet and the TTC, the weekly grocery haul was a severe test of my physical endurance. Every time I came out of the supermarket, both my hands were full of heavy plastic bags, the handles digging deep into my fingers. The walk back to the dorm felt like a painful, loaded cross-country trek.</p><p>I finally couldn't take it anymore. I decided I needed a small trolley for groceries.</p><p>My first choice, naturally, was the one and only Canadian Tire—a name that sounds like it has everything.</p><p>The store was huge, like a warehouse. It sold everything from car tires and barbecue grills to pots, pans, and gardening tools. I wandered around like a headless fly for a long time before finally finding my coveted trolley in an inconspicuous corner.</p><p>It looked unremarkable—a simple metal frame with a black canvas bag. I flipped over the price tag.</p><p>$29.99.</p><p>Thirty bucks? For such a flimsy-looking little trolley? My "broke international student" DNA screamed an alarm once again. That price could get me two hearty Kabab meals.</p><p>But the thought of no longer having to endure the pain of my fingers being cut into by plastic bags made me grit my teeth and buy it.</p><p>Filled with the joy of an impending "major quality of life upgrade," I pulled my brand-new trolley along on the way back to the dorm. However, about halfway there, I suddenly heard a soft plop.</p><p>I looked down and saw a box of cereal I had just bought, lying upright on the sidewalk.</p><p>I froze. How... how did it get out?</p><p>Puzzled, I inspected the canvas bag. And then, at the bottom of the bag, I found a hole—not too big, not too small, but just large enough for a box of cereal to make a precision "prison break."</p><p>My mind went blank. I had just bought it! It hadn't even completed a single full mission, and it had already deserted its post?</p><p>A surge of anger shot through me. I put the surviving cereal box back in the bag (cupping the bottom with my hand) and, fuming, dragged the "maimed" trolley all the way back to Canadian Tire.</p><p>I was mentally prepared for a three-hundred-round battle with the staff. After all, back home, trying to return a used item usually involved a complex war of words.</p><p>I walked up to the customer service counter and handed over the trolley and the receipt.</p><p>"Hi, I'd like to return this. There's a hole at the bottom," I stated my case concisely.</p><p>The lady at the counter didn't even bother to inspect the hole. She just took the receipt, typed a few things on her computer, and then asked, "Cash or back to your card?"</p><p>"Back to my card, please."</p><p>"Okay, all done."</p><p>The entire process took less than a minute. It was so fast it felt unreal.</p><p>I walked out of Canadian Tire with the successful refund receipt, my heart filled with mixed feelings. On one hand, I was amazed by the "lightning speed" and "no-questions-asked" return policy; on the other, I was speechless at their appallingly poor product quality.</p><p>From then on, I basically never bought anything that needed to be load-bearing or durable from Canadian Tire again.</p><p>After all, I had no desire to re-live the "surprise" of picking up my cereal from the sidewalk.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="15"><p>Long before I came to Toronto, I had heard that our university's list of electives included some very "peculiar" options, like African drumming and color exploration courses. I thought at the time, this university is quite the Renaissance institution, teaching everything.</p><p>But I never would have imagined that the real "Renaissance" wasn't on the course schedule at all, but was hidden in the basement of the Student Center we passed by eight hundred times a day.</p><p>The discoverer of this earth-shattering secret was, naturally, our dorm's most well-informed and gossipy "intelligence center"—Karen.</p><p>That night, she burst into our common area like a gust of wind, her face glowing with the excitement of someone who had discovered a new continent. She said to us mysteriously, "Girls! Guess what I found!"</p><p>"What? The school cafeteria released new products?" I asked without looking up.</p><p>"No!" she waved her hands excitedly. "There is a huge arcade hidden under the Student Center in our school!"</p><p>Leia, Anne, and I looked at each other, our faces all showing the same disbelief. Under the Student Center? I did remember a staircase leading to the basement, but the fire door at the bottom was always closed. I couldn't imagine what was hidden behind it.</p><p>"Why would I lie to you!" Karen stomped her foot, seeing our disbelief. "I went to the parking garage to see my friend today and saw it with my own eyes! There was everything in there!"</p><p>And so, under her vivid and persuasive "kidnapping," the three of us, half-believing, followed her like a group of secret agents on an expedition, marching towards the Student Center.</p><p>We crossed the noisy food court on the first floor, went around a few students who were studying, and found an inconspicuous staircase leading to the basement. The stairwell was dimly lit, with only a few emergency lights on. The further down we went, the more we could hear a muffled, rhythmic music and the din of voices.</p><p>When we pushed open the heavy fire door in the basement, a vibrant, colorful new world, completely different from the academic atmosphere upstairs, instantly exploded before our eyes.</p><p>It really was a huge arcade!</p><p>Dazzling neon lights flickered in the dim space, and the air was filled with a peculiar mix of popcorn and electronic components. The loud music, the ding-ding-ding of the arcade machines, and the cheers and laughter of people all mingled into a wave of heat that washed over us.</p><p>The four of us were stunned, standing at the entrance like four country bumpkins who had stumbled onto a movie set.</p><p>This place was an entertainment paradise! Along one wall was a whole row of flashing dance machines, with a few skilled players stomping out dazzling moves. On the other side were several gleaming bowling lanes, with the crisp sound of pins being struck echoing from time to time.</p><p>Further in, there were several pool tables with green felt tops, where a few guys were bent over, aiming their shots with professional-looking stances. But what surprised me the most was in the quietest corner of the arcade, where there were actually a few chessboards. Two calm middle-aged men were playing chess, completely absorbed and unbothered by the surrounding noise.</p><p>Action and stillness, noise and contemplation, modern and classic—all coexisted so harmoniously in this secret underground space.</p><p>It was then that I truly felt that I had only seen the tip of the iceberg of my university. It was like a giant castle full of hidden rooms and secret passages. You never know what kind of surprising new world you'll discover behind the next inconspicuous door.</p><div class="ep-photos"><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep56-campus-signs.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep56-campus-signs.jpg" alt="A wayfinding sign on campus" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>A wayfinding sign on campus.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep56-campus-sunset.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep56-campus-sunset.jpg" alt="Campus at sunset" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>Campus at sunset.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="16"><p>Besides playing Genshin Impact and other miHoYo games (like Honkai: Star Rail), my CBC friend, Ivy, was a hardcore card player through and through.</p><p>"Card player," or "Pái lǎo," was a new term I learned specifically for Ivy. It refers to players who are utterly obsessed with trading card games.</p><p>Don't be fooled by Ivy's usual quiet, introverted demeanor. She was gentle and polite in class and would always be reading or drawing quietly in the library. But the moment the topic turned to card games, a mysterious switch inside her would flip, and she'd become more extroverted than me, talking nonstop with a sparkle in her eyes.</p><p>And I, as her best friend, unfortunately (or perhaps, fortunately) became the chief audience for her passion and her designated "shopping buddy."</p><p>Almost every month, on one or two weekends (or sometimes on a Friday when I had no class), Ivy would message me: "New pack is out. Downtown?" This usually wasn't a question, but a notification.</p><p>And so, I would be "dragged" from the dorm by her onto the TTC and taken all the way to a card shop hidden on a downtown street corner.</p><p>That shop was different from any other I had ever been to. The air was a mixture of the smell of old paper and plastic, and the narrow aisles were flanked by floor-to-ceiling shelves crammed with all sorts of card boxes I couldn't understand.</p><p>The shop was always crowded with various "card players," mostly guys, gathered in small groups, either battling fiercely or pointing at the rare cards in the glass display cases, muttering "spells" I couldn't comprehend—"Synchro," "Xyz," "Link Summon."</p><p>But the moment Ivy stepped inside, she was like a fish back in water. She would greet the owner skillfully and then dive headfirst into the boxes of _Yu-Gi-Oh!_ cards to begin her sacred "pack opening" ritual.</p><p>I, on the other hand, felt like a Muggle who had stumbled into a secret society gathering, just standing aside and watching her carefully tear open the packs and examine the cards one by one.</p><p>"Oh my god! Look at this Starlight Rare!" she would suddenly whisper, excitedly holding up a card shimmering with a strange light for me to see.</p><p>I'd lean in to look. It would be a cool-looking dragon or a handsome magician, with a background so sparkly it dazzled my eyes. All I could do was nod and praise it with my limited vocabulary: "Wow, so shiny! So cool!"</p><p>However, the real "torture" was yet to come.</p><p>Ivy had an almost obsessive ritual for her cards. She believed that every important card deserved a perfectly color-coordinated sleeve and binder. So, after buying the cards, we would move on to the next phase: picking out new "outfits" for her "newly acquired spouses."</p><p>She would hold up a "Blue-Eyes White Dragon" card, walk over to a wall full of colorful card sleeves, and fall into deep thought. First, she'd pick up a pack of sky-blue sleeves, hold it against the card, and shake her head. Then, she'd pick up a pack of royal-blue ones, hold it against the card again, her brow furrowing even deeper.</p><p>"Syna, which one do you think is better?" she would ask, holding up two packs of what looked to me like identical blue sleeves, looking at me with an extremely serious expression, as if I were making a decision of global importance.</p><p>"Umm... the right one?" I would venture.</p><p>"Really? But don't you think this one matches the blue in its eyes more?" she would immediately retort, before falling into a new round of indecision.</p><p>And so, for the color coordination of just a few cards, we could spend over an hour in that tiny shop. I went from giving serious advice at the beginning, to giving perfunctory answers, and finally to giving up on thinking altogether, just blankly repeating: "Yeah, this one looks great."</p><p>I often felt that I might never understand why a piece of cardboard could make Ivy so obsessed.</p><p>But every time I saw her finally choose the perfect color, carefully slide the card into its new sleeve, and wear that contented smile, like a child who just got candy, I felt like I understood everything.</p><p>It wasn't just a card. It was her passion, her world, a shining, unbreachable castle she had built for herself in this bustling city.</p><p>And I was honored to be the one she pulled along to help guard it.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="17"><p>Okay, I admit that girls have a lot to gossip about, but I never expected my roommates to be this into it.</p><p>After the first few days of cautiously getting to know each other, our dorm's "nightly talks" began. It usually happened on a night when no one felt like studying. The four of us would just collapse on the couch in the common area and share stories from our day.</p><p>As the only Chinese person in the dorm, my new roommates were naturally full of curiosity about this "mysterious species" from the Far East.</p><p>"Syna, do you have a boyfriend?" One night, Karen, our resident gossip queen, fired the first shot while munching on chips.</p><p>"No," I said, shaking my head.</p><p>"Oh!" Karen's eyes lit up instantly, like Columbus discovering the New World. "Then what's your type? You know, tall, dark hair, and handsome? Or the funny guy?"</p><p>Leia and Anne immediately looked over too, their curious gazes joining Karen's. Three pairs of eyes were now fixed on me, full of expectation.</p><p>My mind went completely blank.</p><p>Type?</p><p>Seriously? How could a hardcore otaku like me be interested in real-life guys? The images flashing through my mind weren't of any 3D human males, but a long line of my "2D husbands": the handsome psycho with white hair and red eyes, the cool-faced guy with black hair and golden eyes, or the green-haired bard who looks harmless but is full of mischief…</p><p>How was I supposed to explain to them that my type was a man who could wield divine power and summon giant stone spears from the sky?</p><p>"Well..." I cleared my throat, trying to describe my cross-dimensional taste in a language they could understand. "I like... um... hair with a special color, like white or blond. And the eye color... preferably brown or black. As for personality, I like someone who looks cool and doesn't talk much, but is actually very gentle on the inside..."</p><p>I stammered my way through the description, while the three of them listened with intense seriousness.</p><p>"Oh, I see!" Karen slapped her thigh, a look of sudden understanding on her face. "So you like the K-pop idol type! Like, from BTS or Stray Kids?"</p><p>"Or maybe like a character from Twilight?" Leia added, blinking her big eyes. "You know, pale skin, golden eyes, and super strong."</p><p>No, girls, you don't get it. It's not the same thing at all, my mind was screaming.</p><p>Seeing my lukewarm reaction to their examples, Anne, always the observant one, asked carefully in a very gentle and inclusive tone:</p><p>"Or... maybe you're into girls? That's totally cool, you know."</p><p>At that moment, my brain went into full-on meltdown.</p><p>I looked at Anne's sincere, kind eyes, then at Karen and Leia, who were having their own eureka moments and gearing up for a whole new line of gossip. I felt like the dimensional wall between us wasn't just thick; it had undergone a spatial warp, teleporting me to a universe I had never anticipated.</p><p>How could I possibly explain? Was I supposed to say, "No, I'm not into girls, I'm just into non-existent, illustrated boys"? That sounded about ten thousand times weirder than just admitting I liked girls!</p><p>I gave up trying to explain. I just nodded with an awkward smile, pretending they were all correct.</p><p>And my silent agreement completely ignited their gossip-loving souls.</p><p>And so, an international symposium on the topic of "What is Syna's Type?" officially commenced in our small common area. The conference lasted from ten at night until one in the morning. For three solid hours, they analyzed everyone from Hollywood actors to lead singers of European indie bands; from the "visuals" of K-pop boy bands to handsome TikTok influencers.</p><p>Karen even opened her Tinder and started swiping through profiles as if she were an empress choosing a consort, asking me to comment on the men.</p><p>And whenever I showed the slightest hesitation or lack of interest, Anne and Leia would immediately pivot the discussion to "all the cool and beautiful girls," from supermodels to actresses to guitarists in all-female bands.</p><p>I felt like a prisoner under interrogation, only able to respond with bland phrases like, "Hmm, maybe..." and "He/She is cute." My soul had long since departed and was now soaring over the continent of Teyvat.</p><p>That night, their discussion evolved from "Which K-pop star best fits Syna's aesthetic?" to "Should we throw a Pride party in our dorm?"</p><p>As for me, I quietly returned to my room and opened my phone.</p><p>Looking at the handsome man from Teyvat on my lock screen, I let out a long sigh of relief.</p><p>Alright, Syna, it looks like your "type" is destined to become a permanent, beautiful, and increasingly ridiculous misunderstanding in this dorm.</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="18"><p>Besides my hometown's plum rain season, I never expected that tens of thousands of kilometers away in Canada, there would also be a kind of sticky, suffocating "plum rain" weather.</p><p>Summer is supposed to be Canada's most beautiful season. Generous sunshine, azure skies, even the breeze is refreshing. But sometimes, it transforms and becomes as clingy as the cold winter. On those days, the air is filled with moisture, the sky is perpetually grey, and the whole world feels like it's been covered with a damp, not-fully-wrung-out towel—oppressive and gloomy.</p><p>Then, one afternoon, a downpour comparable to a typhoon descended without warning. Huge raindrops smashed against the windowpanes with a loud patter, and the sky turned as dark as ink, as if the end of the world had arrived early.</p><p>After the storm, the sky was still overcast and hadn't cleared up. Feeling suffocated by the stuffy air in the dorm, I decided to go for a walk on campus.</p><p>The post-rain campus air carried a fresh scent of mixed grass and earth. Puddles dotted the ground, reflecting the heavy, lead-grey sky. I walked slowly by myself, my mood as gloomy as the weather.</p><p>Just as I was walking past the lawn in front of the library with my head down, I happened to look up, and then, I froze.</p><p>Against the oppressive, grey canvas of the distant sky, a huge, vividly colored rainbow had quietly appeared at some point.</p><p>It was so clear, so complete, like a giant, seven-colored arch spanning between two academic buildings. The bright red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet, like a miracle, suddenly cut through the gloomy world, so beautiful it was almost unbelievable.</p><p>I stood there, staring at it blankly, feeling my breath grow lighter.</p><p>Then, I discovered something even more amazing.</p><p>Next to that incredibly clear rainbow, there was a faint, secondary arc with its colors reversed, like a shy, pale shadow nestled quietly beside it.</p><p>It was a double rainbow.</p><p>I took out my phone, wanting to capture this rare sight. But no matter how I adjusted the angle, I couldn't fully capture its magnificence and the magical atmosphere. I decided to just put my phone away. But, not wanting to give up, I still raised my camera and took a photo that didn't quite seem to capture the mood.</p><p>I stopped thinking about my headache-inducing essays, and I stopped thinking about the unresolved uncertainties of the future. I just stood there, quietly, greedily, using my eyes to memorize this fleeting, miraculous moment.</p><p>I don't know if seeing a double rainbow truly means good luck.</p><p>But at least in that moment, I felt that it was a gentle, silent comfort, a gift from this city that was sometimes maddening, and at other times, full of surprises.</p><figure class="ep-photo"><button type="button" class="ep-photo-open" data-ep-photo-open data-src="/images/toronto-100/ep66-double-rainbow.jpg" aria-label="Zoom in"><img src="/images/toronto-100/ep66-double-rainbow.jpg" alt="The double rainbow that appeared that day" loading="lazy" /></button><figcaption>The double rainbow that appeared that day.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div class="ep-lightbox" data-ep-lightbox hidden><button type="button" class="ep-lightbox-close" data-ep-lightbox-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button><img data-ep-lightbox-img src="" alt="" /></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="20"><p>What’s here is only a few selections. The full collection, <em>100 Small Things About Toronto</em>, is available on Amazon:</p><p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FJ777PP8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">English edition · Amazon →</a></p><p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FHT2MGXW" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Traditional Chinese edition · Amazon →</a></p></div></div><p><a href="/en/writing/">← Back to Writing</a></p></article></Base>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
  document.querySelectorAll('.ep-layout').forEach((layout) => {
    const tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    const panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    const orderedTabs = [...tabs].sort((a, b) => Number(a.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]) - Number(b.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]));
    const tabContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-tabs');
    const panelContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-panels');
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => tabContainer.appendChild(tab));
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => panelContainer.appendChild(layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]')));
    tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
    panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active'));
    orderedTabs[0].classList.add('active');
    layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + orderedTabs[0].dataset.ep + '"]').classList.add('active');
    const getStats = (panel) => {
      const text = [...panel.querySelectorAll('p')].map((paragraph) => paragraph.textContent).join(' ');
      const words = (text.match(/\S+/g) || []).length;
      const minutes = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(words / 200));
      return `${words.toLocaleString('en-US')} words \u00b7 about ${minutes} min read`;
    };
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => {
      const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
      panel.querySelectorAll('.ep-title, .ep-reading-meta').forEach((el) => el.remove());
      const stats = getStats(panel);
      const panelTitle = document.createElement('h2');
      panelTitle.className = 'ep-title';
      panelTitle.textContent = tab.textContent.trim();
      const panelMeta = document.createElement('div');
      panelMeta.className = 'ep-reading-meta';
      panelMeta.textContent = `${stats} \u00b7 Essay`;
      panel.prepend(panelMeta);
      panel.prepend(panelTitle);
    });
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'));
        tab.classList.add('active');
        const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
        panel.classList.add('active');
        window.requestAnimationFrame(() => panel.classList.add('entering'));
        panels[0].closest('.ep-panels').scrollTop = 0;
      });
    });
  });
});

  document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
    var target = event.target;
    var openBtn = target.closest('[data-ep-photo-open]');
    if (openBtn) {
      var layout = openBtn.closest('.ep-layout');
      var box = layout && layout.querySelector('[data-ep-lightbox]');
      var img = layout && layout.querySelector('[data-ep-lightbox-img]');
      if (box && img) { img.src = openBtn.getAttribute('data-src') || ''; box.hidden = false; }
      return;
    }
    var closeBtn = target.closest('[data-ep-lightbox-close]');
    if (closeBtn || target.hasAttribute('data-ep-lightbox')) {
      var openLightbox = target.closest('.ep-layout') && target.closest('.ep-layout').querySelector('[data-ep-lightbox]');
      if (openLightbox) openLightbox.hidden = true;
    }
  });
  document.addEventListener('keydown', function (event) {
    if (event.key !== 'Escape') return;
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-ep-lightbox]').forEach(function (box) { box.hidden = true; });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .prose:has(.ep-layout) { max-width:1000px; }
